const faqs = [
  { q: 'What is the Best Answer Hub Bank Statement to Excel converter?', a: 'The Best Answer Hub Bank Statement to Excel converter is a free, browser-based tool that turns a PDF bank statement into a clean Excel or CSV file. It separates the date, description, and amount columns, checks the running balance, and runs entirely on your device, so the statement is never uploaded to a server.' },
  { q: 'Is it really free, with no signup or limits?', a: 'Yes. It is completely free, with no account, no email, no credits, no page or row caps, and no watermarks. Many converters charge a subscription or per-page fee and gate the download behind a login. This tool has none of that, because it runs on your device with no server cost to recover.' },
  { q: 'Does it upload my bank statement to a server?', a: 'No. Every step runs in your browser, so the PDF never leaves your device and is never stored or seen by anyone else. You can confirm it by opening the browser network tab while converting: no file data is sent. The tool even keeps working after you disconnect from the internet.' },
  { q: 'How do I convert a PDF bank statement to Excel?', a: 'Open the Best Answer Hub tool, drag in your PDF statement, and review the detected table. The converter rebuilds the rows and runs a running-balance check, highlighting anything that does not reconcile. Choose Excel or CSV and download. The whole process takes a few seconds for a typical statement.' },
  { q: 'Can I convert a bank statement for QuickBooks or Xero?', a: 'Yes. Export to CSV, the most widely accepted format, and import it into QuickBooks, Xero, or another accounting or budgeting tool. The converter builds clean, standard columns for date, description, money out, money in, and balance, which import software reads far more reliably than a raw PDF.' },
  { q: 'Is it safe to convert a bank statement online?', a: 'Yes. The Best Answer Hub converter is safe to use because the conversion runs in your browser and the file is never transmitted. A bank statement holds account numbers, balances, and every transaction, so it is among the most sensitive files you own. Nothing is uploaded, so there is no server copy to expose.' },
  { q: 'Why is my older bank history only available as a PDF?', a: 'Banks cap direct downloads to a recent window, often 60 to 90 days or up to 12 to 24 months, and keep older months as PDF statements only. To rebuild a longer history in a spreadsheet, download those PDFs and convert each one, which is the exact job the Best Answer Hub tool is built for.' },
  { q: 'Does it work on scanned or photographed statements?', a: 'It works best on text-based PDFs, the kind a bank generates digitally. Scanned or photographed statements have no text layer, so the tool detects them and offers best-effort text recognition, which is slower and less accurate. For the cleanest result, download the original digital PDF from your bank.' },
  { q: 'How accurate is the conversion?', a: 'On clean, text-based statements, most rows convert correctly, and the running-balance check flags anything that does not add up. Accuracy drops on unusual multi-column layouts, international formats, and scanned files. The tool is designed to be honest about this: it shows a preview and highlights rows to review rather than hiding errors.' },
  { q: 'Can it handle separate debit and credit columns and multi-line descriptions?', a: 'Yes. Separate money-out and money-in columns are detected by their position on the page and kept apart, and single signed-amount layouts are handled too. Descriptions that wrap onto a second line are folded back into one row instead of creating a phantom transaction, a common failure of basic converters.' },
  { q: 'How is it different from DocuClipper or MoneyThumb?', a: 'Those tools are capable but typically upload your statement to a server or need a paid desktop install, and require an account or subscription. The Best Answer Hub converter is free, runs in your browser so nothing is uploaded, needs no account, and has no page limits, with a running-balance check you can verify in the preview.' },
  { q: 'Can accountants use it for confidential client statements?', a: 'Yes. Because the file is processed on the device and never uploaded, a client statement does not land on an outside server, which suits bookkeepers and accountants bound by client-confidentiality and data-protection duties. The running-balance check and editable preview also help the numbers reconcile before they reach a ledger.' },
  { q: 'Can I edit the data before downloading?', a: 'Yes. The converter shows an editable preview after reading the statement, so you can correct any cell, fix a flagged row, or tidy a description before exporting. This keeps you in control of the final spreadsheet rather than trusting a black-box output that may hide errors until reconciliation.' },
  { q: 'Does it work on a phone?', a: 'Yes. The tool runs in any modern mobile browser on iPhone and Android, with no app to install. Open the page, pick a PDF from your files, and convert. Very large or scanned statements process more slowly on a phone, so a laptop is faster for long documents, but all of it is mobile-friendly.' },
  { q: 'Which banks does it support?', a: 'It is format-aware rather than bank-specific, reading statements by detecting the column layout instead of relying on a fixed template per bank. Common single-column layouts convert most reliably. Unusual regional, credit-union, or international formats may need a quick review of the preview and small edits before download.' },
];

const seo = {
  title: 'How to Convert a Bank Statement to Excel Free (2026)',
  description: 'Convert a PDF bank statement to clean Excel or CSV, free and entirely in your browser. Nothing uploaded, no signup, plus how to unlock older history.',
